  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 149 AM MDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nThe last two nights I used music to describe the weather of the day. \nSo, why not make it three days in a row. Let's step back \nanother decade today, to the 60s. And let us also change music \ngenres again, this time to country. And this has to with a \npattern that you would expect more during the middle of summer, \nnot a week from the start of autumn. The song is by the late, \ngreat Johnny Cash, Ring Of Fire. Basically, a strong ridge sets \nup in the southern United States and moisture rides along the \nnorthern edge of it.\n\nThere continues to be a strong ridge over the southeastern and \nsouth central United States, bringing more record high \ntemperatures. For us in Wyoming, we will be close to the top of \nthe ridge, with an increasing chance of showers. For today and \nmost of tomorrow, the bulk of the moisture looks to remain to \nthe south of Wyoming. Just enough moisture will creep northward \nlate today and especially tonight to bring some showers to the \nsouthern third of the state. But these still look fairly \nisolated and even here most locations should not see one (less \nthan a 1 out of 4 chance). Moisture will try to creep further \nnorth on Wednesday but even on this day the bulk will remain \nsouth of the border with only isolated activity. As for \ntemperatures, today looks fairly close to normal. With southerly\nflow increasing Wednesday, temperatures look warmer with highs \nrising to around 80 in some of the warmer spots.\n\nChances of showers should increase beginning Wednesday night and \nespecially into Thursday and Friday. The ridge over the southeastern \nUnited States with retrograde toward the southern plains. At the \nsame time, low pressure over the Pacific Northwest will begin to get \npushed eastward. moving toward Idaho by Thursday night. This will \npush the plume of monsoonal moisture further north and west and into \nthe Cowboy State. Precipitable water values could be rather high. \nThe GFS has values approaching an inch, which is approaching 200 \npercent of normal Wednesday night and Thursday. This model does tend \nto overcook this somewhat, so reality may be closer to 150 percent \nof normal. Nevertheless, the chance of showers and storms will \nincrease. Model guidance continues to have disagreements in the \nplacement of the most numerous showers though. So, we have higher \nconfidence in Thursday and Friday being the two more active days, \nbut placement of the most numerous showers and precipitation amounts \nremains uncertain. Temperatures will remain somewhat above normal, \nalthough with more cloud cover a bit cooler than on Wednesday. And \nlike most monsoonal events, all areas will have a chance of a shower \nor storm, but not everyone will see one.\n\nThere is more uncertainty for the weekend. There is still a decent \nmodel spread on how fast the low will exit the area. Most guidance \nnow takes the low north of the area across Montana. With flow \nturning northwestward, Saturday looks like a cooler day across the \narea. How fast the low moves will determine the extent of any \nprecipitation though since it will be of the wrap around variety. \nMost guidance favors northern Wyoming given the position of the low, \nwith lesser chances further south. Sunday looks somewhat drier \nalthough a few areas may still see a few showers around. Ridging \nshould build back over the area to start next week, bring a return \nto dry and seasonable weather.",
